//! crypto/ed25519_scalarmul.rye -- Season G (Cryptography), the eighth rung: scalar multiplication on edwards25519 authored in pure Rye,
//! composed entirely over the GREEN group law (crypto/ed25519_group.rye), which itself stands over the GREEN base field
//! (crypto/fe25519.rye). Ed25519 signing -- the Lotus signed carry, Kumara identity, Vault, Comlink -- computes the public key A = a-B and the
//! commitment R = r-B by adding the base point to itself a scalar number of times; this file authors exactly that operation, k-P, as a
//! composition over the group's two primitives, point doubling and point addition. It adds no new field math and no new group math; it is the
//! tractable, highest-Lindy move that turns a proven group into a proven scalar multiplication -- the last arithmetic rung before Ed25519's
//! sign and verify can be assembled.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography - Rye-native, Monocypher-parity, audit-ready).
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md (Season G names Ed25519 as the Lotus
//! unblock; its scalar multiplication is a composition over the group law) and the group law's own header, crypto/ed25519_group.rye, whose
//! closing GREEN line names scalar multiplication as its next composition.
//! Kin: crypto/ed25519_group.rye (the group this composes over, GREEN) - crypto/fe25519.rye (the base field beneath it, GREEN). Monocypher
//! (vendor/monocypher, CC0/BSD-dual) is the parity TARGET, never a copied line -- our own composition, studied clean-room from the public
//! mathematics of twisted Edwards curves and Monocypher's public API (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
//!
//! THE CRUX -- a scalar k times a point P is P added to itself k times, which the double-and-add ladder computes in a number of group
//! operations proportional to the bit length of k rather than to k itself: walk the scalar's 256 bits from most significant to least, and at
//! each bit double the running accumulator, then add P when that bit is set. The accumulator starts at the group identity (X = 0, Y = 1,
//! Z = 1, T = 0). Because the base point has prime order L, this agrees with any other correct scalar multiplication on the same integer
//! multiple, so a 32-byte scalar above L simply lands on (k mod L)-P -- the same point a reduced scalar would. The one product with the
//! outside world is the scalar's little-endian byte order, exactly the order Ed25519 uses for both secret scalars and the encoded challenge.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE -- software only, purely LOCAL, no network, no funds, no real device, no identity. A scalar and a curve point are arithmetic,
//! not a secret and not a signature: this file signs nothing, holds no key, and calls no std.crypto in the primitive. Ed25519's signing with
//! an identity key stays its own separate, gated round; this rung only proves that k-P is computed correctly. This double-and-add ladder
//! branches on the scalar's bits and so is deliberately VARIABLE-TIME -- correct, not yet timing-safe; a constant-time ladder for secret
//! scalars stays a separate named horizon, the same horizon the whole Season carries. Parity is proven at selftest against two references
//! runnable now -- algebraic known-answers anyone can check (0-B is the identity, 1-B is B, 2-B is the doubling of B, (k+1)-B is k-B plus B),
//! and Zig's own std.crypto.ecc.Edwards25519.mul as an independent implementation, compared byte-for-byte on the compression of k-P across a
//! spread of scalars and across both the base point and one of its multiples. Monocypher-source parity stays the same held-fetch horizon the
//! other primitives carry.
//!
//! Run from the repository root:
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run crypto/ed25519_scalarmul.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;
const fe = @import("fe25519.rye");
const pt = @import("ed25519_group.rye");

pub const ed25519_scalarmul_version = "20260815.201500";

/// The maximum number of scalar bits the ladder walks -- a full little-endian 256-bit scalar, the width Ed25519 encodes. Named so the loop
/// bound is a stated constant rather than a literal buried in the walk.
pub const scalar_bits: u32 = 256;

/// The group identity in extended twisted-Edwards coordinates: x = 0, y = 1, so X = 0, Y = 1, Z = 1, T = XY/Z = 0. The neutral element the
/// double-and-add accumulator starts from -- adding it changes nothing, doubling it stays itself.
pub fn identity() pt.Point {
    const r = pt.Point{
        .x = fe.Field{ .l = .{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 } },
        .y = fe.Field{ .l = .{ 1, 0, 0, 0, 0 } },
        .z = fe.Field{ .l = .{ 1, 0, 0, 0, 0 } },
        .t = fe.Field{ .l = .{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 } },
    };
    // invariant: the identity carries y = 1 and x = 0 exactly -- the neutral element, not merely some point.
    assert(r.y.l[0] == 1 and r.x.l[0] == 0);
    // invariant: Z is one, so the affine reading y = Y/Z is Y itself with no inversion needed here.
    assert(r.z.l[0] == 1);
    return r;
}

/// Scalar multiplication k-P by the double-and-add ladder: walk the 32-byte little-endian scalar s from its most significant bit down to its
/// least, doubling the accumulator at every bit and adding P wherever the bit is set. Composes only pt.dbl and pt.add over the GREEN group
/// law -- no field inversion, no new curve math. Deliberately variable-time (it branches on scalar bits); constant-time is the named horizon.
pub fn scalarmul(p: pt.Point, s: *const [32]u8) pt.Point {
    // invariant: the ladder walks exactly scalar_bits bits, high to low -- the bound is a named constant, not an open loop.
    assert(scalar_bits == 256);
    var r = identity();
    var i: u32 = scalar_bits;
    while (i > 0) {
        i -= 1;
        // invariant: the byte index stays inside the 32-byte scalar (i < 256 so i >> 3 < 32), and the bit index fits three bits.
        assert((i >> 3) < 32 and (i & 7) <= 7);
        r = pt.dbl(r);
        const byte = s[i >> 3];
        const bit = (byte >> @intCast(i & 7)) & 1;
        if (bit == 1) {
            r = pt.add(r, p);
        }
    }
    // invariant: the result is a well-formed extended-coordinate point -- its limbs stay inside the reduced range the group law maintains.
    assert(r.z.l[0] <= 0x7ffffffffffff and r.y.l[0] <= 0x7ffffffffffff);
    return r;
}

// ---- selftest ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn bytes_eq(a: []const u8, b: []const u8) bool {
    if (a.len != b.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < a.len) : (i += 1) {
        if (a[i] != b[i]) return false;
    }
    return true;
}

/// Build a 32-byte little-endian scalar from a small unsigned value -- the low eight bytes carry v, the rest are zero. Used to name small
/// known-answer scalars (1, 2, 5, ...) plainly inside the selftest.
fn le32(v: u64) [32]u8 {
    var s = [_]u8{0} ** 32;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    var acc = v;
    while (i < 8) : (i += 1) {
        s[i] = @intCast(acc & 0xff);
        acc >>= 8;
    }
    return s;
}

/// Independent reference: Zig's own std.crypto edwards25519, used ONLY inside the selftest as a second implementation to prove our ladder
/// matches byte-for-byte. The primitive above never calls it.
const ZEd = std.crypto.ecc.Edwards25519;

/// Copy a Zig point into our representation through the canonical byte form of each coordinate, so parity compares two genuinely independent
/// implementations on the same projective representative -- the same bridge the group law's selftest uses.
fn from_zig(zp: ZEd) pt.Point {
    const xb = zp.x.toBytes();
    const yb = zp.y.toBytes();
    const zb = zp.z.toBytes();
    const tb = zp.t.toBytes();
    return pt.Point{ .x = fe.from_bytes(&xb), .y = fe.from_bytes(&yb), .z = fe.from_bytes(&zb), .t = fe.from_bytes(&tb) };
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    const b = ZEd.basePoint;
    const pb = from_zig(b);

    // 1. Algebraic known-answers anyone can check.
    {
        // 0-B is the identity (Y = 1, all-else zero).
        var id_b = [_]u8{0} ** 32;
        id_b[0] = 1;
        if (!bytes_eq(&pt.to_bytes(scalarmul(pb, &le32(0))), &id_b)) return error.ZeroScalarMismatch;

        // 1-B is B itself.
        if (!bytes_eq(&pt.to_bytes(scalarmul(pb, &le32(1))), &b.toBytes())) return error.OneScalarMismatch;

        // 2-B is the doubling of B.
        if (!bytes_eq(&pt.to_bytes(scalarmul(pb, &le32(2))), &pt.to_bytes(pt.dbl(pb)))) return error.TwoScalarMismatch;

        // (k+1)-B equals k-B + B, for a small k -- the ladder is consistent with a single extra addition.
        const k: u64 = 20;
        const kb = scalarmul(pb, &le32(k));
        if (!bytes_eq(&pt.to_bytes(scalarmul(pb, &le32(k + 1))), &pt.to_bytes(pt.add(kb, pb)))) return error.SuccessorMismatch;
    }

    // 2. Independent-implementation parity: our ladder equals Zig's std.crypto.ecc.Edwards25519.mul byte-for-byte on the compression of k-P
    //    across a spread of scalars, over both the base point and one of its multiples (b3 = 3-B, still a prime-order-subgroup point).
    {
        const small = [_]u64{ 1, 2, 3, 7, 16, 255, 1000, 65537 };
        // A fixed large scalar with a small top byte -- well under 2^252, never zero, never a multiple of L, so Zig's mul rejects nothing.
        const big = [32]u8{
            0x9d, 0xb6, 0x1c, 0x0f, 0x27, 0x44, 0x8e, 0x11, 0xa3, 0x5c, 0x02, 0x77, 0xd8, 0x41, 0x63, 0x9a,
            0x0e, 0x5b, 0xc7, 0x30, 0x88, 0x12, 0x6f, 0xa9, 0x44, 0xd1, 0x3e, 0x07, 0x91, 0x2a, 0x60, 0x0e,
        };

        const b3 = b.dbl().add(b); // 3·B, an independent prime-order point to multiply as well as the base point.
        const points = [_]ZEd{ b, b3 };

        for (points) |zp| {
            const p = from_zig(zp);
            for (small) |v| {
                const s = le32(v);
                const ours = pt.to_bytes(scalarmul(p, &s));
                const theirs = (try zp.mul(s)).toBytes();
                if (!bytes_eq(&ours, &theirs)) return error.RefSmallScalarMismatch;
            }
            const ours_big = pt.to_bytes(scalarmul(p, &big));
            const theirs_big = (try zp.mul(big)).toBytes();
            if (!bytes_eq(&ours_big, &theirs_big)) return error.RefBigScalarMismatch;
        }
    }

    print("GREEN crypto-ed25519-scalarmul: scalar multiplication on edwards25519 authored in pure Rye — Season G's eighth rung, composed " ++
        "entirely over the GREEN group law (crypto/ed25519_group.rye) and, beneath it, the GREEN base field (crypto/fe25519.rye). k·P is " ++
        "computed by the double-and-add ladder: walk the 32-byte little-endian scalar from its high bit down, double the accumulator at " ++
        "every bit and add P where the bit is set, starting from the group identity (X = 0, Y = 1, Z = 1, T = 0). It composes only point " ++
        "doubling and point addition — no field inversion, no new curve math. The ladder is deliberately variable-time (it branches on " ++
        "scalar bits), so it is correct but not yet timing-safe; a constant-time ladder for secret scalars stays a separate named horizon. " ++
        "The primitive calls no std.crypto. Parity holds against two references runnable now: algebraic known-answers (0·B is the identity, " ++
        "1·B is B, 2·B is the doubling of B, (k+1)·B is k·B plus B), and our authored Rye equals Zig's independent " ++
        "std.crypto.ecc.Edwards25519.mul byte-for-byte on the compression of k·P across a spread of scalars over both the base point and " ++
        "3·B. Monocypher's own scalar multiplication passes the same mathematics — its vendored source is a held fetch, so " ++
        "Monocypher-source parity is an honest horizon rung. Purely local arithmetic — a scalar and a point are not a secret and not a " ++
        "signature, so no real key, no signing, no network, no funds, no real device; Ed25519's signing with an identity key stays its own " ++
        "separate, gated round. The group now multiplies by a scalar, and Ed25519's public-key derivation A = a·B and commitment R = r·B " ++
        "become compositions over this ladder.\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init_args: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init_args;
    return run_selftest();
}
